  "host": "WSL2",
  "command": "make bench-bandwidth",
  "methodology": "Device-to-device copy of a 1 GiB buffer, timed with CUDA events. Bytes moved per copy are 2 * buffer (one read and one write), which is what the spec-sheet GB/s number counts. Each sample times 100 back-to-back copies, because a single copy is short enough for the memory clock to drop and this RTX 3070 is also driving the display. One untimed sample warms up, then ten timed samples; the ceiling uses the median. Clocks are read while a copy series is queued, and once more immediately after the last sample. That second read can still see boost; the idle clock is the sample taken before any copy. Clocks are not locked. The decode ceiling is batch * bandwidth / step_bytes, where a step reads every streamed weight once, one embedding row per sequence, and the live KV of every sequence (K and V, context includes the new token). Activations are not in the byte count. The 1.7B fp32 ceiling is arithmetic only: those weights are about 6.8 GB and do not fit beside a CUDA context on this 8 GB card. Host is WSL2.",
